^.*invariant violation.*$
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
KNOWNBUG: std::erase_if over a populated std::vector is currently a no-op, so
the size assertion fails (the vector keeps all three elements).

The crash this call used to trigger (a convert_function return-type
exception-safety bug) is fixed and separately regression-tested by
cpp20_erase_if_return_type, so this test no longer aborts CBMC -- it now runs to
a clean assertion FAILURE.

Remaining functional gap: std::erase_if's body does
`__cont.erase(std::__remove_if(__cont.begin(), __cont.end(), __pred),
__cont.end())`.  While erase_if's (system-header) body is converted, member
lookup of `erase` on the `vector<int>&` parameter resolves to an empty
candidate set ("found no match for symbol 'erase'"), so the body throws and is
discarded under the system-header guard, leaving erase_if a no-op.  Direct use
of vector::erase(first, last) in user code (where the vector instance is fully
elaborated at the point of lookup) works, so this is an ordering/availability
gap in member-function visibility during a dependent template body's conversion
([temp.inst]/2-4 on-demand member instantiation), not a missing model.

Reclassify CORE once erase_if's body converts (vector::erase is found and
instantiated on demand) so the populated vector shrinks and size() == 2 holds.
